  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when you put in the effort to add schema markup, validate it, and then your rich results don't show up. You're not alone! While structured data makes your page eligible for those cool rich snippets in search results, it doesn't actually guarantee they'll appear. Google has its own criteria, and sometimes even perfect markup might not get displayed. Think of it like a job application: you might have all the qualifications, but the company still decides who gets the interview.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\nOne big factor is whether your markup accurately reflects what's visible on the page. If you're marking up content that isn't actually there for users to see, or if it's inaccurate, Google will likely ignore it. They're looking for genuine, helpful content that matches your structured data. Also, the type of schema you're using matters. Google supports specific features for certain types, so make sure you're aligning with their recommendations. For instance, using `FAQPage` for actual question-and-answer pairs is much more effective than trying to force it onto a regular article.\n\nAnother thing to consider is the overall quality and authority of your site. While schema helps AI understand your content, it won't magically make a low-quality page rank. Google's algorithms still prioritize useful, well-written content from trustworthy sources. They also want to ensure a good user experience, so if your page is slow or buggy, that could impact whether rich results are displayed.\n\nSometimes, it's just about timing. After you deploy new schema, it can take a while for Google to recrawl and process the changes. Don't expect instant results, but do monitor your Search Console reports for any errors or warnings. If you have a complex site with dynamic content, multiple templates, or a recent migration, that's often when professional help becomes super important. An expert can pinpoint tricky issues and ensure your schema strategy aligns with Google's evolving guidelines. This isn't a one-and-done task; it's an ongoing effort to keep your site speaking Google's language clearly. By getting it right, you make it easier for AI systems to understand and potentially cite your business, making you the answer when prospects ask questions.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
